\section{The order, and why it is not a choice}{

The evaluation order is \strong{query, then transport, then fit}. Query and
transport act on different axes of the same array --- the query mixes
experimental coordinates, the transport mixes spatial nodes --- so they
commute exactly, and under a subject-constant weight operator the fit
commutes with both. What is \emph{not} free is which may run first: participants
have different native frames, so there is no common node axis to fit at
before transporting, and fit-then-transport is not definable at all. The
order is recorded on the plan rather than selected here.

Reading the query first is what makes the route cheap. Complete geometry at
a native node is \eqn{q(q+1)/2}{q(q+1)/2} packed coordinates; a bank of
\code{K} queries is \code{K} numbers, and complete geometry is never allocated.
}

\section{What the result is a ledger of}{

\code{component} selects which part of each participant's conservative geometry
is carried, and the transported object takes its own name
(\code{population-form-v1} section 8.1) because it is not the group-node quantity
the bare name would suggest.\tabular{lll}{
   \code{component} \tab \verb{$ledger} \tab what it is \cr
   \code{"total"} \tab \code{transported_total} \tab a genuine group-node total for that participant \cr
   \code{"coherent"} \tab \code{native_coherent_ledger} \tab native-node coherent evidence carried to a group node \cr
   \code{"configuration"} \tab \code{native_configuration_ledger} \tab native-node configuration evidence carried to a group node \cr
}


\code{native_coherent_ledger + native_configuration_ledger = transported_total}
holds exactly; what fails is reading the coherent ledger as a group-node
common mode. Transport carries \emph{nodes}, so there is no group frame and no
group-node weight vector for such a mode to be defined against.

The sink is a row of the result, labelled \verb{<sink>}, not a discarded
remainder: it is fitted like any other column because covariate-linked sink
mass is how a differentially failing transport becomes visible. It is never
a value at a location, and it is always reported in budget units.
}

\section{Budget preservation}{

Under \code{"budget"} semantics each participant's transported total, sink
included, equals its native total exactly. The certificate is asserted per
participant and per query at fit time, with a tolerance of \code{1e-12} times the
ledger's \eqn{L^1}{L1} norm --- not its total, which is a signed sum that
may sit near zero while its summands are large. A participant that fails it
has an ill-formed transport, and the run refuses rather than renormalizing.
}

\section{Normalization}{

\code{"none"} fits the ledgers as they are, so participants with more evidence
weigh more. \code{"unit_budget"} divides each participant's ledger by its own
native total first, so each contributes budget one; the divisor is a
\emph{signed} estimate, so a participant whose total is not bounded away from
zero by \code{budget_floor} is marked \code{NA} for that query rather than emitting a
divergent share. The default \code{budget_floor = 0} admits every nonzero total,
which is the weakest honest criterion and is recorded as such in the
receipt: the constant is an open maintainer decision, and it needs a
per-participant standard error that does not exist yet. Both normalizations
are computed against a total read from the same data as the ledger, which
the receipt declares (\code{budget_estimate = "same_data_ratio"}).
}

\section{Uncertainty}{

\verb{$uncertainty} always carries \verb{$between}, the ingredients of the
between-subject layer: the group design's unscaled covariance
\eqn{(X'X)^{-1}}{(X'X)^-1} and the terms it is indexed by. That is what
\code{\link[=population_uncertainty]{population_uncertainty()}} needs and the result does not otherwise hold;
the standard errors themselves are computed when they are read rather than
stored as three more \code{node}-by-\code{query}-by-\code{term} arrays.

Supplying \code{uncertainty} adds two more blocks. \verb{$native} is D8's own
per-native-node covariance per participant, carried \strong{untransported}, with
\verb{$transported} recording the refusal: a group node's value mixes native
nodes, so its covariance needs the covariance \emph{between} them, and no route
produces one. \verb{$within} is the part of that refusal E8 lifts --- a group
column fed by exactly one native row, where the cross-node terms carry
weight zero --- admitted per participant and per column and absent
elsewhere. No independence assumption is made anywhere.

The two layers are reported separately and are never pooled; there is no
field holding their sum. See \code{\link[=population_uncertainty]{population_uncertainty()}}.
}

